@@ -11,7 +11,18 @@ Repositories, code search, issues, pull requests and users through GitHub's offi
 
 Health check: `GET https://api.github.com/user`.
 
+## Results
+
+Tool results reach agent transcripts, so they don't carry the credentials GitHub puts in some responses. REST and MCP results go through the same filter in `results.ts`:
+
+- `temp_clone_token` and `temp_download_token`, the short-lived credentials on repository and runner-download reads, are dropped wherever they appear.
+- A file's `download_url`, and a raw-content link in a message from GitHub's MCP server, lose their `token` query parameter, which reads a private file without the account. Read the file through the tools instead. File contents, issue bodies and other text stay as GitHub sent them.
+- A secret-scanning alert's `secret`, the leaked value itself, is dropped.
+- A credential minted because a person asked for one, such as a runner registration token or an installation token, is returned only by the REST tools `results.ts` lists as minting it. Every other result drops those fields.
+
 ## Known gaps
 
 - Release asset upload (`uploads.github.com`) is skipped.
+- The five operations under `/applications/{client_id}` are left out: checking, resetting, scoping and deleting a token, and deleting an authorization. GitHub answers them only for an OAuth or GitHub app's own client ID and secret, sent as Basic credentials, which a connected account does not have.
+- Operations that act as an app, not a person, are still listed, such as those under `/app` and the Marketplace listing. GitHub answers them for a GitHub App's JWT, so they fail with a connected account's token.
 - The MCP server serves its default toolsets; the REST API covers the rest. MCP tools and annotations were not listed with a real account.
